# =============================================================================
# Line endings.
#
# Development happens on Windows with core.autocrlf=true, and deployment targets Linux. Without this
# file, what lands in a working tree depends entirely on each machine's local git config — which is
# fine for source, and NOT fine for anything the kernel or a container has to execute.
#
# A shell script checked out with CRLF has a carriage return in its shebang, so Linux tries to run the
# interpreter "/usr/bin/env bash\r" and fails with:
#
#     bash: ./backup-db.sh: /usr/bin/env: bad interpreter: No such file or directory
#
# which names neither the file nor the real cause. The same \r at the end of any `RUN` line breaks a
# Dockerfile build in a similarly unhelpful way. Both are cheap to prevent and expensive to diagnose,
# and the backup/restore runbook depends on those scripts running on the VPS.
#
# Everything else keeps the default: git normalises to LF in the repository and hands Windows whatever
# its autocrlf setting asks for.
# =============================================================================

* text=auto

# Must be LF in the WORKING TREE, on every platform — these are executed, not just read.
*.sh            text eol=lf
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
*.Dockerfile    text eol=lf
.env.example    text eol=lf

# Windows-only, must stay CRLF.
*.ps1           text eol=crlf
*.bat           text eol=crlf
*.cmd           text eol=crlf

# Never touch these — normalising a binary corrupts it.
*.png           binary
*.jpg           binary
*.jpeg          binary
*.gif           binary
*.ico           binary
*.pdf           binary
*.jar           binary
*.gz            binary
*.zip           binary
*.woff          binary
*.woff2         binary
*.ttf           binary
*.eot           binary
*.mp4           binary
